1
00:00:01,730 --> 00:00:14,120
 "Changer notre planète, ces arbres ont fait de vous l'homme que vous êtes.

2
00:00:14,120 --> 00:00:23,440
 Ils ont tout dit, la lune qui gravite, et la pomme qui tombe déconfite.

3
00:00:23,440 --> 00:00:33,010
 Ils ont qui donnent l'heure, ils ont vous offré tous les gages du bonheur.

4
00:00:33,010 --> 00:01:06,170
 Une lune spèche chemin aux rêves des humains, se suive ton filet au chimette."

5
00:01:06,170 --> 00:01:28,240
 6th 1945, Almagordo, New Mexico.

6
00:01:28,240 --> 00:01:39,230
 Seven, six, five, four, three, two, one, fire!

7
00:01:44,790 --> 00:01:50,330
 Big bomb! Much brighter than a thousand suns!

8
00:01:50,880 --> 00:01:52,060
 Lord, where are we headed?

9
00:01:54,190 --> 00:01:56,750
 Hiroshima, August 6, 1945.

10
00:01:57,770 --> 00:02:00,710
 And that day...

11
00:02:00,710 --> 00:02:04,990
 Hiroshima, ten o'clock, it's exactly ten seconds, we drop it!

12
00:02:06,140 --> 00:02:14,580
 Nine, eight, seven, six, five, four, three, two, one, let her go!

13
00:02:14,800 --> 00:02:40,530
 And man had now unleashed a terrifying power, a destructive force that he hardly understood.

14
00:02:40,630 --> 00:02:42,750
 A few years earlier, he hadn't even known it existed.

15
00:02:43,150 --> 00:02:48,410
 Well, sure, the atomic bomb ended a horrible war, but it also changed everybody's life.

16
00:02:48,930 --> 00:02:50,180
 Just who invented it?

17
00:02:50,690 --> 00:02:52,410
 It was more than one person.

18
00:02:53,870 --> 00:02:56,090
 Lots of men and women worked to make it.

19
00:03:00,530 --> 00:03:10,890
 Well, if I had to choose one scientist who contributed to the discoveries, well, um...

20
00:03:10,890 --> 00:03:16,220
 Today?

21
00:03:16,770 --> 00:03:18,220
 Yes, sir, I think I am.

22
00:03:18,700 --> 00:03:20,080
 You think you are?

23
00:03:20,240 --> 00:03:20,680
 Yes, sir.

24
00:03:20,680 --> 00:03:22,800
 You're not sure?

25
00:03:22,970 --> 00:03:26,350
 Well, sir, it's hard to be sure of anything.

26
00:03:26,550 --> 00:03:31,210
 Silence! You'll write I'm sure I'm in class 100 times. Understand?

27
00:03:31,570 --> 00:03:33,070
 Yes, sir. I think so.

28
00:03:33,600 --> 00:03:35,380
 You think so? You're not sure?

29
00:03:35,660 --> 00:03:38,410
 Yes, sir. I'm sure. I think.

30
00:03:39,050 --> 00:03:42,050
 200 times! You'll write it 200 times! Understand?

31
00:03:42,810 --> 00:03:45,070
 What do I write 200 times, sir?

32
00:03:45,600 --> 00:03:48,440
 You'll write what I told you to write. Understand?

33
00:03:48,940 --> 00:03:51,060
 No, no, don't say any more, Albert.

34
00:03:51,530 --> 00:03:56,170
 As long as you know you're in Munich, Germany, and what we want is discipline.

35
00:03:56,490 --> 00:03:58,250
 Oh, walk straight. An arrow.

36
00:03:59,270 --> 00:04:01,810
 No, don't answer. Just go and walk straight.

37
00:04:07,050 --> 00:04:16,570
 Watch it, Al. He told you to walk straight.

38
00:04:17,340 --> 00:04:19,260
 He'll kick you out.

39
00:04:19,320 --> 00:04:22,440
 Oh, you know teachers. They think they're generals. Walk straight.

40
00:04:22,560 --> 00:04:25,120
 But if you do, you'll never discover anything.

41
00:04:25,660 --> 00:04:29,510
 Water is made of two atoms of hydrogen and one of oxygen.

42
00:04:29,830 --> 00:04:30,390
 That clear?

43
00:04:32,210 --> 00:04:33,300
 Yes.

44
00:04:33,810 --> 00:04:35,970
 And you, Albert Einstein, you said nothing?

45
00:04:36,360 --> 00:04:37,560
 Is all of that clear?

46
00:04:37,780 --> 00:04:38,960
 Yes, sir. I suppose it is.

47
00:04:39,440 --> 00:04:41,470
 That's all? That's all suppose?

48
00:04:42,110 --> 00:04:44,650
 Well, you see, sir, it's not easy to be certain.

49
00:04:44,830 --> 00:04:48,610
 Einstein, get out for nothing!

50
00:04:48,610 --> 00:05:02,400
 Look here, Albert. It won't do expelled from school.

51
00:05:02,400 --> 00:05:06,130
 I wasn't learning anything, Pop. Just a lot of discipline.

52
00:05:06,130 --> 00:05:09,470
 Stand up, walk straight, shut up, sit down.

53
00:05:09,470 --> 00:05:12,500
 In Germany, discipline counts a lot, I guess.

54
00:05:12,500 --> 00:05:16,350
 One of your relatives lives in Zurich, Switzerland. You could go to school there.

55
00:05:16,350 --> 00:05:21,210
 I really like Switzerland. All the mountains and the chocolate? Great!

56
00:05:21,210 --> 00:05:22,850
 How well your mother plays.

57
00:05:22,850 --> 00:05:28,050
 I've got a new mathematical riddle, the kind that you like.

58
00:05:28,050 --> 00:05:29,390
 Here it is.

59
00:05:29,390 --> 00:05:34,980
 Now, the animal that we're looking for is mysterious, though we'll call him X, but we'll catch him, you'll see.

60
00:05:42,560 --> 00:05:49,980
 Okay?

61
00:05:51,680 --> 00:06:05,200
 Over there, this is a boy's school.

62
00:06:05,880 --> 00:06:07,830
 Yeah, she must have gotten lost.

63
00:06:09,030 --> 00:06:11,490
 She thinks this is where she's going to learn sewing.

64
00:06:11,730 --> 00:06:12,910
 I don't know how to be a nurse.

65
00:06:13,690 --> 00:06:17,310
 There's no reason why a girl can't study physics and mathematics.

66
00:06:17,530 --> 00:06:18,450
 Don't make fun of her.

67
00:06:18,950 --> 00:06:20,220
 And your own business.

68
00:06:20,880 --> 00:06:21,960
 Oh, you'll regret it, Albert.

69
00:06:22,370 --> 00:06:24,530
 Listen, you're lucky they're holding me.

70
00:06:24,730 --> 00:06:25,990
 Nobody's holding you back.

71
00:06:26,860 --> 00:06:28,680
 Nobody wants to hear your ideas.

72
00:06:28,800 --> 00:06:30,340
 She's got no business in our school.

73
00:06:38,760 --> 00:06:39,480
 Albert Einstein.

74
00:06:39,820 --> 00:06:40,740
 Maleva Merich.

75
00:06:41,260 --> 00:06:44,020
 I came here to study theoretical physics.

76
00:06:44,180 --> 00:06:45,600
 Me too, and mathematics.

77
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:47,420
 So do I, and astronomy.

78
00:06:48,050 --> 00:06:49,720
 So do I. It's curious.

79
00:06:50,180 --> 00:06:51,440
 Good, we'll be seeing each other.

80
00:06:51,500 --> 00:06:51,840
 Uh-huh.

81
00:06:52,130 --> 00:06:55,130
 Lately, there have been so many remarkable discoveries in physics.

82
00:06:55,490 --> 00:06:57,030
 The movement of molecules.

83
00:06:57,430 --> 00:06:58,070
 And electrons.

84
00:06:58,350 --> 00:06:59,190
 Forces of inertia.

85
00:06:59,390 --> 00:07:00,270
 And of gravitation.

86
00:07:07,230 --> 00:07:10,740
 Look at it.

87
00:07:11,200 --> 00:07:12,780
 Maleva, you know...

88
00:07:12,780 --> 00:07:14,130
 Know what, Albert?

89
00:07:14,130 --> 00:07:21,130
 Well, the moon is 240,000 miles away, and light from it takes one second to reach us.

90
00:07:21,550 --> 00:07:23,550
 On Earth, we see it one second late.

91
00:07:23,830 --> 00:07:24,550
 Well, so what?

92
00:07:25,320 --> 00:07:31,990
 Imagine, a ray of light, leaving the sun, reflecting off the moon, and then coming to Earth.

93
00:07:32,360 --> 00:07:35,610
 Imagine that I could travel at the speed of light.

94
00:07:35,890 --> 00:07:37,070
 Do you think that's possible?

95
00:07:37,600 --> 00:07:40,130
 I'm not sure, but let's suppose that it is.

96
00:07:41,930 --> 00:07:45,900
 It looks like a man, a little man.

97
00:07:46,630 --> 00:07:47,670
 It's not possible.

98
00:07:48,150 --> 00:07:52,170
 No, a man can't go as fast as us to what he calls nobody, that's right.

99
00:07:52,230 --> 00:07:53,810
 Unless it's all in a dream, huh?

100
00:07:54,260 --> 00:07:58,270
 If I move at the speed of light, there won't be any more light for me to see

101
00:07:58,270 --> 00:08:00,010
 because relative to me, it doesn't move.

102
00:08:00,250 --> 00:08:01,930
 It's getting late. Let's go home.

103
00:08:33,310 --> 00:08:40,160
 It was you, Albert. Again, you have forgotten your key, I suppose.

104
00:08:40,620 --> 00:08:43,420
 I'm sorry, Mrs. Koontz. I guess I was lost in thought.

105
00:08:43,980 --> 00:08:47,780
 You are doing the same thing, Albert. You have head in the clouds.

106
00:08:48,670 --> 00:08:50,850
 Yeah, good. I come right down.

107
00:08:50,850 --> 00:09:02,360
 Sure is complicated. Your idea, there's lots easier things to think about, Albert.

108
00:09:02,810 --> 00:09:08,120
 Really, it's simple. Look, I'm saying it's impossible to determine absolute motion for any object.

109
00:09:08,340 --> 00:09:10,980
 Imagine the Earth turns around the sun, huh?

110
00:09:11,410 --> 00:09:12,420
 Well, sure it does.

111
00:09:13,000 --> 00:09:15,570
 But the sun is moving in our galaxy.

112
00:09:16,550 --> 00:09:17,810
 That's right, it does.

113
00:09:18,420 --> 00:09:20,380
 Well, then what is the Earth's motion?

114
00:09:21,420 --> 00:09:28,620
 Some scientists say the universe is filled with a very light substance called ether and all the planets move through it.

115
00:09:28,620 --> 00:09:33,470
 But I don't believe that. We'll do an experiment to find out.

116
00:09:35,710 --> 00:09:38,780
 Wait, this goes on the top.

117
00:09:38,780 --> 00:09:41,360
 We're going to measure movement of the air.

118
00:09:41,360 --> 00:09:42,860
 Of course, it's not ether.

119
00:09:42,860 --> 00:09:45,250
 Careful, Albert.

120
00:10:02,720 --> 00:10:32,930
 I knew it would end up bad.

121
00:10:32,930 --> 00:10:48,730
 You can say it proved a lot.

122
00:10:48,730 --> 00:10:52,160
 All the same, I'm sure that ether doesn't exist.

123
00:10:52,160 --> 00:11:11,070
 Interesting. So light does have a force.

124
00:11:11,250 --> 00:11:11,670
 Right.

125
00:11:11,790 --> 00:11:12,910
 And it's made up of particles.

126
00:11:13,630 --> 00:11:15,560
 Suppose we call them photons.

127
00:11:20,100 --> 00:11:22,990
 Watch it. We're coming in.

128
00:11:23,290 --> 00:11:24,250
 The photon's energy.

129
00:11:24,370 --> 00:11:25,650
 Produces an electric current.

130
00:11:25,850 --> 00:11:27,150
 We could calculate its energy.

131
00:11:27,370 --> 00:11:29,110
 Unexplained photoelectricity.

132
00:11:31,750 --> 00:11:36,860
 Now what are they talking about?

133
00:11:37,520 --> 00:11:39,180
 Who cares? It's all useless.

134
00:11:41,720 --> 00:11:42,660
 Useless is it?

135
00:11:42,810 --> 00:11:43,830
 Well, take a look.

136
00:11:46,420 --> 00:11:51,090
 Without photons, no TV or photos.

137
00:11:51,590 --> 00:11:52,790
 In fact, no more light.

138
00:11:53,200 --> 00:11:55,680
 And so the atomic bomb was Einstein's?

139
00:11:55,680 --> 00:11:57,750
 No, not really.

140
00:11:58,190 --> 00:12:05,460
 But he discovered a law of physics that caused a sensation, the transformation of mass into energy, without that idea.

141
00:12:05,640 --> 00:12:07,000
 Einstein discovered everything.

142
00:12:07,430 --> 00:12:11,330
 No, not quite everything. And we mustn't forget that Milevo was a big help.

143
00:12:12,370 --> 00:12:14,310
 An object can be turned into energy.

144
00:12:14,670 --> 00:12:18,560
 And its mass is equivalent to a tremendous quantity of energy. Incredible.

145
00:12:18,560 --> 00:12:21,760
 This small object can release a colossal energy.

146
00:12:22,160 --> 00:12:24,220
 The equivalent of tons of dynamite.

147
00:12:26,120 --> 00:12:28,380
 Here's the formula. E equals mc squared.

148
00:12:28,480 --> 00:12:31,460
 What an elegant formula. Can mankind use this energy?

149
00:12:31,850 --> 00:12:34,350
 They'll use it all right, but it really worries me.

150
00:12:35,790 --> 00:12:38,580
 Albert, I found you a job.

151
00:12:39,070 --> 00:12:41,190
 That's great. I really need one.

152
00:12:41,920 --> 00:12:43,530
 Is it a teaching job?

153
00:12:43,610 --> 00:12:46,650
 Uh, no. It's at the Patton's office in Bern.

154
00:12:47,400 --> 00:12:48,670
 What'll I do there?

155
00:12:49,440 --> 00:12:54,160
 Well, you look at what people invent and decide whether their gadgets are useful or not.

156
00:12:54,520 --> 00:12:56,140
 Anyway, you'll be ready on the living.

157
00:12:56,550 --> 00:12:57,820
 I appreciate it, Marcel.

158
00:12:58,760 --> 00:13:00,520
 Mileva, we're moving to Bern.

159
00:13:00,700 --> 00:13:14,120
 Well, Mr. Einstein, the least we can say is you do work quickly.

160
00:13:14,780 --> 00:13:16,540
 Albert, what time is it?

161
00:13:16,900 --> 00:13:18,490
 The time, yes.

162
00:13:19,380 --> 00:13:21,310
 It's 10 o'clock! We'll be late!

163
00:13:21,990 --> 00:13:24,950
 I have to change!

164
00:13:27,470 --> 00:13:37,150
 Where'd I put it?

165
00:13:38,570 --> 00:13:51,360
 I haven't forgotten the keys.

166
00:13:52,750 --> 00:13:55,110
 Don't have them.

167
00:14:01,660 --> 00:14:07,380
 And you forgot Zim again!

168
00:14:07,480 --> 00:14:08,960
 I'm sorry, I really am.

169
00:14:09,720 --> 00:14:11,680
 You just stay there! I'll come.

170
00:14:15,910 --> 00:14:24,550
 You know, I think our theory's taking shape.

171
00:14:24,890 --> 00:14:26,620
 Come and help me with some calculations.

172
00:14:27,870 --> 00:14:28,790
 Yes, all right.

173
00:14:29,010 --> 00:14:30,610
 And you come and stir the soup.

174
00:14:32,460 --> 00:14:32,820
 Baby.

175
00:14:35,620 --> 00:14:55,520
 Albert is learning to walk.

176
00:14:58,330 --> 00:14:58,810
 Wonderful!

177
00:14:59,910 --> 00:15:02,390
 Mileva, this theory of relativity's almost ready.

178
00:15:02,390 --> 00:15:12,540
 Do you think that the speed of light is certain?

179
00:15:12,890 --> 00:15:14,100
 Time is relative.

180
00:15:18,190 --> 00:15:19,710
 Is that not relative always the same?

181
00:15:19,750 --> 00:15:20,370
 Not at all.

182
00:15:21,620 --> 00:15:23,420
 It depends on the observer's viewpoint.

183
00:15:23,970 --> 00:15:25,750
 Don't give a tick for your observer.

184
00:15:27,220 --> 00:15:30,810
 Count the seconds, the minutes, the years, the centuries.

185
00:15:31,760 --> 00:15:32,740
 Time is not elastic.

186
00:15:33,770 --> 00:15:34,950
 Yes, it is.

187
00:15:35,030 --> 00:15:35,770
 Just the word.

188
00:15:35,990 --> 00:15:36,750
 It's elastic.

189
00:15:38,330 --> 00:15:43,450
 Let's suppose that in the future, we'll send rockets into space, moving at the speed of light.

190
00:15:43,790 --> 00:15:46,930
 Let's also imagine that I can split into two people.

191
00:15:46,930 --> 00:15:51,770
 And my double goes off aboard one of the rockets.

192
00:16:03,010 --> 00:16:04,670
 186,000 miles per second.

193
00:16:04,870 --> 00:16:06,470
 That's almost the speed of light.

194
00:16:25,440 --> 00:16:48,750
 Years have elapsed on the rocket at almost 186,000 miles a second.

195
00:16:49,070 --> 00:16:50,370
 Almost no time's gone by.

196
00:16:51,990 --> 00:16:54,070
 We're stood still, so?

197
00:16:54,450 --> 00:16:56,640
 So I only aged a couple of years.

198
00:16:57,060 --> 00:17:00,180
 Had I stayed on Earth, I too would be 40 years older.

199
00:17:00,640 --> 00:17:03,600
 Means that after such a trip, you're younger than I am.

200
00:17:04,140 --> 00:17:05,270
 That's right.

201
00:17:05,530 --> 00:17:07,770
 It's only a theoretical experiment, of course.

202
00:17:07,770 --> 00:17:12,070
 No rocket can move at the speed of light.

203
00:17:12,720 --> 00:17:13,880
 Anyway, not yet.

204
00:17:15,440 --> 00:17:16,780
 These Einsteins are crazy.

205
00:17:17,710 --> 00:17:19,230
 Not as crazy as all that.

206
00:17:20,120 --> 00:17:25,550
 In 1905, Einstein published four articles that attracted worldwide attention.

207
00:17:26,170 --> 00:17:27,730
 Of course, not everyone agreed.

208
00:17:28,490 --> 00:17:31,240
 Don't pay any attention to Einstein and his relativity.

209
00:17:32,040 --> 00:17:33,790
 The whole idea is absurd.

210
00:17:36,780 --> 00:17:40,960
 The replay in the burnt patent office isn't going to teach all the scientists in Germany.

211
00:17:42,020 --> 00:17:47,140
 There's a survey meeting, a gathering of the greatest scientists from around the world.

212
00:17:47,140 --> 00:17:55,120
 Max Planck, Langerin, Rutherford, Perrin, Marie Curie, Poincaré, and the youngest of all, well, it was Einstein.

213
00:17:56,140 --> 00:18:00,680
 In 1913, Einstein is named director of the Kaiser Wilhelm Institute in Berlin.

214
00:18:01,160 --> 00:18:05,380
 Lever refused to go with him. In any case, their marriage was on the rocks.

215
00:18:06,920 --> 00:18:11,380
 Shortly after the divorce, Albert married his cousin, Elsa.

216
00:18:12,780 --> 00:18:17,660
 Just as Einstein was awarded the Nobel Prize, the greatest scientific award there is,

217
00:18:17,660 --> 00:18:23,010
 A spirit. A Jewish plot.

218
00:18:24,000 --> 00:18:25,380
 That's right, Professor Weyland.

219
00:18:25,930 --> 00:18:28,340
 That's true, and I'll explain why.

220
00:18:28,930 --> 00:18:45,310
 This theory is no more than a bad joke.

221
00:18:46,960 --> 00:18:50,280
 Time maintains that light, that time.

222
00:18:50,740 --> 00:18:52,860
 Well, you see how suppersive it is.

223
00:18:53,120 --> 00:18:54,940
 Excuse me, I forgot my pen.

224
00:18:55,230 --> 00:18:56,470
 Would you lend me one?

225
00:18:57,300 --> 00:18:58,910
 With pleasure, Professor.

226
00:18:59,840 --> 00:19:02,810
 German good sense opposes these.

227
00:19:03,070 --> 00:19:05,290
 Flights of fantasy. Time is unchanging.

228
00:19:05,690 --> 00:19:06,990
 Space is infinite.

229
00:19:08,030 --> 00:19:11,220
 Relativity has nothing to do with Germans or Jews.

230
00:19:11,780 --> 00:19:13,500
 It's a universal law.

231
00:19:13,960 --> 00:19:18,010
 Consider space, time, and that, gentlemen, is what relativity is.

232
00:19:23,210 --> 00:19:31,700
 Oh, in Einstein's hand, my pen will describe the entire universe, and it's mine.

233
00:19:34,430 --> 00:19:37,940
 They're all dangerous.

234
00:19:42,590 --> 00:19:46,290
 Einstein was right.

235
00:19:46,560 --> 00:19:47,280
 You see that?

236
00:19:49,080 --> 00:19:52,830
 Einstein had predicted it.

237
00:19:53,130 --> 00:19:56,050
 A gravitational field is distorted by the sun's mass.

238
00:19:56,490 --> 00:19:59,030
 Light rays, therefore, are bent towards the sun.

239
00:20:00,090 --> 00:20:03,120
 Proves the theory of relativity is correct.

240
00:20:03,120 --> 00:20:09,770
 This letter was signed by a hundred of the greatest scientists in Germany.

241
00:20:10,520 --> 00:20:12,440
 All of them condemn Einstein's relativity.

242
00:20:12,700 --> 00:20:14,700
 Because there are a hundred doesn't make them right.

243
00:20:15,600 --> 00:20:15,840
 Come.

244
00:20:37,300 --> 00:20:40,790
 Hitler came to power.

245
00:20:41,160 --> 00:20:44,920
 For him, Jews were the enemies of the state and Einstein was at the top of the list.

246
00:20:48,540 --> 00:20:58,320
 Einstein is dismissed and he leaves Germany for the United States.

247
00:21:02,080 --> 00:21:06,840
 This is Einstein, professor.

248
00:21:07,260 --> 00:21:10,020
 May I welcome you to Princeton University.

249
00:21:10,720 --> 00:21:12,660
 I hope you'll be happy here with us.

250
00:21:14,330 --> 00:21:17,560
 This is your new home.

251
00:21:17,560 --> 00:21:27,580
 There's a big book you're reading.

252
00:21:27,980 --> 00:21:29,150
 Guess you know how to count.

253
00:21:30,040 --> 00:21:30,810
 Mm-hmm.

254
00:21:31,520 --> 00:21:34,590
 You can give me some help.

255
00:21:35,180 --> 00:21:37,370
 I've got a hard problem to do for my homework.

256
00:21:37,900 --> 00:21:39,330
 Well, let me see.

257
00:21:39,410 --> 00:21:42,100
 Two plus three, that equals...

258
00:21:42,100 --> 00:21:43,470
 And five.

259
00:21:44,050 --> 00:21:44,870
 You sure?

260
00:21:45,270 --> 00:21:47,850
 As sure as you can be about anything.

261
00:21:48,460 --> 00:21:50,200
 We'll see if the teacher agrees.

262
00:22:00,140 --> 00:22:15,870
 You want to know where Einstein lives?

263
00:22:16,250 --> 00:22:16,960
 The professor?

264
00:22:18,150 --> 00:22:20,740
 I'm sorry.

265
00:22:20,960 --> 00:22:23,020
 We're not allowed to give out that kind of information.

266
00:22:24,540 --> 00:22:27,750
 You say that you are the professor Einstein?

267
00:22:28,210 --> 00:22:29,720
 Yes, I am Albert Einstein.

268
00:22:29,720 --> 00:22:31,460
 I forgot my address.

269
00:22:31,950 --> 00:22:33,210
 Would you give her to me, please?

270
00:22:33,210 --> 00:22:33,310
 Oh!

271
00:22:50,210 --> 00:22:52,420
 Smart.

272
00:22:53,410 --> 00:22:54,610
 Yes, I wonder.

273
00:22:56,050 --> 00:22:59,030
 I also have a problem, and I don't know how to solve it.

274
00:22:59,530 --> 00:23:00,140
 Well?

275
00:23:01,090 --> 00:23:06,510
 I'm trying to find the universal law that would link gravity and electromagnetism.

276
00:23:06,650 --> 00:23:08,440
 The law of a unified field, understand?

277
00:23:09,060 --> 00:23:11,200
 Sounds pretty complicated.

278
00:23:11,880 --> 00:23:13,310
 Get over.

279
00:23:15,250 --> 00:23:19,310
 Do I really have to wear one?

280
00:23:19,960 --> 00:23:23,590
 Herbert, when you visit the president, you wear a tie.

281
00:23:24,000 --> 00:23:25,780
 Yes, as usual, you're right.

282
00:23:32,140 --> 00:23:41,260
 President of the United States and Mrs. Franklin D. Roosevelt.

283
00:23:41,440 --> 00:23:48,100
 Sir Einstein, it's a great honor for us.

284
00:23:48,850 --> 00:23:50,150
 We're delighted to welcome you.

285
00:23:50,450 --> 00:23:54,150
 1938, Germany annexes Austria and then takes Czechoslovakia.

286
00:23:54,900 --> 00:23:58,860
 1939, Germany invades Poland, France and Britain enter the war.

287
00:23:59,330 --> 00:24:03,610
 1940, Germany invades Denmark, Norway, the Netherlands, all the way to Paris.

288
00:24:03,830 --> 00:24:08,070
 Italy joins Germany, France invaded, signs an amnesty, and Britain stands alone.

289
00:24:08,070 --> 00:24:20,320
 I detest war, but there are circumstances that justify the use of force when facing an enemy who would destroy the world.

290
00:24:22,130 --> 00:24:30,670
 On December 7th, 1941, the Japanese, who had joined Germany, launched a surprise attack on the American naval base at Pearl Harbor.

291
00:24:31,530 --> 00:24:34,730
 And, of course, the United States entered the war immediately.

292
00:24:46,400 --> 00:24:48,930
 Achieved a chain reaction.

293
00:24:49,330 --> 00:24:50,900
 Now it's only a question of time.

294
00:24:51,390 --> 00:24:51,950
 Look here.

295
00:24:52,170 --> 00:24:55,630
 A neutron hits a uranium nucleus, releasing two or three other neutrons.

296
00:24:55,630 --> 00:24:58,130
 And in turn, these hit other uranium nuclei.

297
00:24:58,490 --> 00:25:00,210
 And thus, a chain reaction is created.

298
00:25:01,160 --> 00:25:05,440
 The energy released corresponds to Einstein's formula, E equals MC squared.

299
00:25:07,460 --> 00:25:09,430
 Squared is the atomic bomb.

300
00:25:10,100 --> 00:25:15,860
 Yes, E is energy and it equals M, the mass of the explosive multiplied by C, the constant speed of light squared.

301
00:25:16,380 --> 00:25:20,210
 But it's also medical radiation treatment that saved many lives.

302
00:25:20,530 --> 00:25:25,050
 Nuclear power plants for supplying electricity as reserves of oil are diminishing.

303
00:25:25,050 --> 00:25:29,280
 And sometime in the future, space travel all the way to distant galaxies.

304
00:25:29,820 --> 00:25:36,060
 Yes, without Einstein's genius, a basic understanding of our universe might never have been revealed.

305
00:25:41,770 --> 00:26:00,570
 *Muchas singing*

